# 25. DEVELOPMENT AND IMPLEMENTATION RULES

This section is mandatory.

You are building from scratch, but you must still avoid unnecessary complexity.

### Rule 1 — Build a complete product, not disconnected screens.

Every important page must connect to real application logic and persistent data.

### Rule 2 — Reuse components.

Do not create duplicate cards, buttons, navigation systems, hooks or API clients.

### Rule 3 — Avoid unnecessary files.

Create a file only when it has a clear architectural responsibility.

Do not generate multiple versions of the same component.

### Rule 4 — Avoid unnecessary dependencies.

Use existing packages whenever possible.

Install new packages only when required.

### Rule 5 — Avoid speculative features.

Do not build complex infrastructure for features that are not required for the MVP.

### Rule 6 — No fake functionality.

Do not display success messages when operations fail.

Do not present mock data as real user data.

Seeded demo data must be clearly identified.

### Rule 7 — Preserve code quality.

Use TypeScript types, reusable services, validation and meaningful error handling.

Avoid giant components and duplicated business logic.

### Rule 8 — Protect the database.

Use migrations and safe updates.

Never reset the database to solve a normal development issue.

### Rule 9 — Control AI usage.

Do not call all agents unnecessarily.

Use event-driven orchestration.

### Rule 10 — Verify before claiming completion.

A feature is complete only when its important user workflow works and relevant checks pass.

